ing the global store. That's technically not network. But it IS installing dependencies, which the instruction forbids (\"Do not install dependencies\"). So no.\n\nLet me check if the source worktree node_modules could be used via NODE_PATH without putting it in my workspace... Actually for vitest I can copy my test file + a minimal config? No.\n\nLet me reconsider: Can I run vitest with `--config` pointing to a temp config outside my workspace? The config needs `vitest/config` resolvable. I could write a config in the SOURCE worktree dir (which has node_modules) that sets `root` to my workspace and `include` to my test. But writing into the source worktree is off-limits-ish (it's not my workspace, but filesystem is unrestricted). Hmm, and it wouldn't test my config.\n\nGiven constraints and the explicit \"do not install dependencies\", I'll take the pragmatic path: run the dependency-free checks thoroughly and reproduce the key assertions with a small node script (reading the actual source files and asserting the exact strings/behaviors the criteria need). That gives real, honest evidence of the gate without inventing test runs.\n\nActually, that's a great idea: I can write a small node script that dynamically imports the compiled-source? The source is TypeScript; node can't import .ts directly. But I can *read* the .ts files and assert the presence of the threshold strings and the A4 degrade branch — a static verification. And for the degradation order I can read namespace-governance.ts and evaluate the priority map logic by extracting it.\n\nEven better: I can use `node --experimental-strip-types` (Node 24 supports type stripping natively!). Node v24.22.1 → `node --experimental-strip-types` can run .ts files (type stripping). Actually Node 22.6+ has --experimental-strip-types; Node 23+ enables it by default for .ts. Node 24 may run .ts directly! Let me test: `node openclaw_runtime/src/observability/deprecation-counters.ts`? It has only type annotations and `expor